From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: damnbitimcrit <333091414+damnbitimcrit@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Kernel] Check file sharing modes and delete pending files as
 the console

Xenia ignored the sharing modes of open files, and its NtOpenFile took
the share access (the fifth argument) as the open options, dropping the
real options. Halo 3 deletes leftover Theater recordings (*.temp in
cache1:\autosave) with DeleteFile at every game start that records, after
it has opened the new recording with no sharing at all: on the console
that delete's open fails with a sharing violation and the recording
survives. In Xenia the delete was marked on the file's shared entry, the
host refused it (the file was open), the mark survived the rename to
.film, and the next close of any handle to it deleted the finished film.

On the console, cache0: and cache1: are served by a file system driver
inside the game's own executable (Halo 3: create sub_82588848,
set-information sub_82589558, cleanup sub_82588560); Xenia serves them
from host folders instead. This follows that driver's rules:

- NtOpenFile takes (handle, access, attributes, I/O status, share
  access, options), as the game calls it.
- With file_share_access (default on, read at each call), NtCreateFile
  checks an open of an existing file on a writable device (not read-only
  devices, device roots or the raw partitions of the NullDevice, whose
  volume opens the utility drive code makes as before), in the console's
  order: a directory opened to be created, replaced or overwritten fails
  with OBJECT_NAME_COLLISION and a file opened as a directory with
  NOT_A_DIRECTORY (creating an existing file and opening a directory as
  a file fail in the open as before); then an open of a file pending
  deletion fails with DELETE_PENDING, and one that conflicts with the
  file's other opens (IoCheckShareAccess: read, write and delete access
  against the sharing they allow, both ways), or replaces a file that is
  open, with SHARING_VIOLATION. Nothing can be created in a directory
  pending deletion (DELETE_PENDING). The open is counted before the host
  open, also while a replace deletes and recreates the file, so a
  concurrent open sees it. The state is kept per file (by path, under
  the global critical region) and follows renames.
- FILE_DELETE_ON_CLOSE needs DELETE access (else INVALID_PARAMETER) and
  marks the file at create, as the console's driver does.
- Setting the delete disposition needs DELETE access on a writable
  device (else ACCESS_DENIED), isn't allowed on a device root
  (INVALID_PARAMETER) and fails with DIRECTORY_NOT_EMPTY for a directory
  that isn't empty. Deleting entries while a directory is enumerated (a
  recursive delete) no longer skips the entry after each one deleted.
- Renaming needs DELETE access, a writable device and a file that isn't
  a device root (else ACCESS_DENIED or INVALID_PARAMETER), and stays on
  the file's device (else NOT_SAME_DEVICE); a target that exists is
  replaced only if the caller allows it (the BOOLEAN's byte alone:
  MoveFile leaves the padding unset) and it isn't a directory (else
  OBJECT_NAME_COLLISION) or open (else ACCESS_DENIED); the file's own
  name is OBJECT_NAME_COLLISION (or ACCESS_DENIED when replacing), a
  target in a directory pending deletion DELETE_PENDING. The checks and
  the host rename, which replaces the target in one step, run under one
  lock, and a file moved to another directory moves there in the tree.
  A host rename that fails is reported (ACCESS_DENIED) and changes
  nothing; before, the entry took the new name anyway.
- A file marked for deletion is deleted when its last handle closes, not
  at the first close.
- A host file being deleted closes its own host handle first, and if the
  host still refuses, the mark is cleared instead of firing at a later
  close; on devices that can't delete (packages, discs) a delete leaves
  the file as it was. Host file handles on Windows share deletion, so a
  guest rename or delete of a file open through other guest handles
  doesn't fail on the host (guest sharing is checked by Xenia).

The first 64 refused opens are logged with the file's open handles.
